CORS is fixed — the chat and brief streams now return `access-control-allow-origin`.

The cause was the SSE path, not the CORS config. `@fastify/cors` was registered correctly with `http://localhost:5173`, but it sets its headers on the Fastify reply object, which only get flushed through Fastify's normal send path. The shared `stream()` helper calls `reply.hijack()` and then writes the status line itself with `reply.raw.writeHead(200, {...})`, so those pending headers were dropped — which is exactly why the browser saw a `200 OK` with no `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` and failed the request anyway.

The fix copies the headers Fastify has accumulated into the raw `writeHead` call:

```27:40:apps/backend/src/api/routes/api/v1/helpers.ts
export const stream = async (
  reply: FastifyReply,
  events: AsyncGenerator<SseEvent>,
) => {
  const first = await events.next();
  reply.hijack();
  // Hijacking skips Fastify's send path, so headers set by hooks (CORS) must be written by hand.
  reply.raw.writeHead(200, {
    ...(reply.getHeaders() as OutgoingHttpHeaders),
    'Content-Type': 'text/event-stream',
    'Cache-Control': 'no-cache',
    Connection: 'keep-alive',
  });
```

Spreading `reply.getHeaders()` rather than hardcoding the origin keeps whatever `@fastify/cors` computed (the echoed origin plus `Vary: Origin`), so it stays correct if `APP_ALLOWED_ORIGINS` changes. Both SSE endpoints share this helper, so `/api/v1/briefs/stream` is fixed too.

Verification: `make typecheck` passes, `make test` is green at 46 tests, and a live `curl` to the running API with `Origin: http://localhost:5173` now returns `vary: Origin`, `access-control-allow-origin: http://localhost:5173`, and `Content-Type: text/event-stream`. The API container picked up the change through the bind mount, so a reload should be enough on your side — no rebuild needed. I also added a one-line note about this in `.cursor/memory/conventions.md` so the hijack/header interaction isn't rediscovered later.
